--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -5,3 +5,36 @@
 Requested periodic behavior: the host decides when each run begins and ends. Perform the first refresh immediately, then wait the supplied positive interval after each successful completion. No overlapping callbacks for a single run. Stop a run on its first callback error and report that error. Stop requests must reach an active callback. The host must be able to wait for completion before freeing resources, and stopping one refresher must not stop another. Each instance has at most one periodic run at a time; concurrent starts/restarts need not be supported. A nonpositive interval is a caller error. The periodic cancellation result must be documented.
 
 This library is one component among several inside the host process. The executable coordinates process termination and resource teardown. Go 1.22 is supported. The API is pre-1.0; add the periodic API while retaining New and Refresh.
+
+## Periodic lifecycle
+
+`Refresher.Start(ctx, interval)` starts an independent periodic run. The first
+callback starts immediately. After each successful callback, the run waits the
+full interval before starting another callback, so calls never overlap. A
+nonpositive interval returns an error. The host supplies a context to control
+the run; call `Stop` to request cancellation and `Wait` to join it. `Stop` does
+not wait. `Wait` returns the first callback error, if any; otherwise it returns
+the context error when the run is canceled (normally `context.Canceled`). A
+callback error wins if it returns an error while cancellation is in progress.
+Callback-captured resources remain host-owned and must stay available until
+`Wait` returns. Starting or restarting concurrent runs on the same Refresher is
+not supported.
+
+The executable should stop every run before waiting on any one run, then wait
+for all of them before releasing shared resources. This lets every active
+callback observe cancellation even when another run has already failed:
+
+```go
+func stopRefreshers(slowRun, fastRun *refreshkit.Run) error {
+	slowRun.Stop()
+	fastRun.Stop()
+	slowErr := slowRun.Wait()
+	fastErr := fastRun.Wait()
+	return errors.Join(slowErr, fastErr)
+}
+```
+
+The example assumes `errors` and the `refreshkit` import. If starting the
+second run fails, stop and wait for the first run before returning the startup
+error. In an executable, call this stop-and-join function from its shutdown
+path before closing any clients or files captured by either callback.
